  "title": "The look you picked, with its edges back — moving, at play scale",
  "question": "This is D (painted) with C's ink line over it, zoomed to 3x, gnome walking and the tree alight. Two things to judge: does the ink crisp it the way you wanted, and are the pale diamonds in the canopy gaps and the undergrowth right, or noise? It ships as a mode you switch to with Shift+=, not as the new default — say if you want it as the default.",
  "context_md": "Same world, same seed, same frames; scrub them together.\n\n- **A** — the game today at 3x.\n- **B** — painted + ink. Soft interiors like D, but each sub-pixel is assigned to what it *is* (air / water / soil / stone / plant) before it is coloured, so a twig is made of twig and never of sky, and an ink line is drawn where a mass meets air.\n\n**The blur was costing you thin things, and that is measured, not a taste claim.** A one-cell diagonal twig under plain D comes through at **0.62-0.90** of its presence today depending on zoom. Under B it is **1.27-1.58** — more present than today, never less. That is the same *\"pixels of plants disappearing\"* you reported when zooming out, arriving from the other end of the zoom range, and it is why the ink is the load-bearing part rather than decoration.\n\n**Also in the selector, on the same key:** illustrated (C on its own — flat fills, no blur) and the chamfer you called the most interesting direction. The chamfer was quietly **deleting** one-cell twigs (0.03 of one at 2x); it no longer does.\n\n**Dials:** `Shift+]` steps the ink weight, and one of its stops turns the ink off entirely so you can see what the rest of it is doing. `Shift+[` is the chamfer's corner rule.\n\nNothing changes at normal zoom — that picture is byte-identical to today under every style.",
